## Four things you do with information

Before the interesting part, the parts list. Everything after this is built out of four operations and the connections between them.

**Observing** — taking in raw signal.
**Structuring** — turning that signal into something coherent. Pattern, shape, relation.
**Organising** — sorting the coherent stuff by what matters and how it connects.
**Communicating** — putting it into a form that can leave you.

And they run in a circle. Observing feeds structuring, structuring feeds organising, organising feeds communicating, and what you communicate changes what you observe next. **The circle closing is what makes it a circle and not a queue**, which turns out to matter more than any of the four.

**Notice what isn't on that list: memory.** It used to be, and taking it off is the biggest change in this version.

Here's why. Memory isn't held in a brain cell. It's held in the *junction* between two brain cells — half of that junction belongs to one and half belongs to the other. So if you examine a single neuron and ask how much memory it has, the honest answer is almost none. Memory only shows up when you look at the pair.

Which generalises: **memory is what a connection looks like over time.** Not a warehouse. A connection that keeps circulating what's been through it. What a good memory buys you isn't more shelf space — it's the same material staying in motion longer.

*Where to go deeper: §1.3.*

