## Two ways to be connected, and they do different jobs

Once you're split, something has to cross the split. There are two kinds of crossing, and they aren't interchangeable.

### The peer kind

Two parts that speak the same language. Either one can start the conversation. Either one could stand in as a picture of the other, because they're doing comparable work on different material.

Your two brain hemispheres are the clean example. Same currency both directions.

Inside the four-part cycle, it's that two-way movement between structuring and organising — the one that runs both directions when nothing else does.

**What this gets you:** a picture of yourself, in your own format, that you didn't produce yourself. And the ability to bind separate streams into one story — which you can only do if they were separate to begin with.

### The world kind

Two parts that speak *different* languages, and can't swap. One turns world into signal. The other turns signal into effect on world.

Your body and your brain. Light hits your eye and becomes something else entirely. A decision leaves your brain and becomes a moving arm.

The test is whether you could run it backwards. You could, in principle, decode brain activity crossing between hemispheres back into what caused it. You cannot look at a nerve firing and recover the light. That one-way lossiness is what makes it the world kind.

**What this gets you — three things, and the third one matters most:**

**An edge.** Where the language changes is where inside meets outside. Two peers chatting in one language have no edge anywhere. Nothing tells them where they stop.

**A limit.** The world is only going to send you so much, in only certain formats. That bounds what you can be. Without it, the thinking part just *runs* — perfectly coherent on the inside, connected to nothing.

**Stakes.** When you're maintaining a boundary and something pushes it, that's not neutral information. It's bad *for you*, natively, before any judgment about it. Hunger isn't a fact you learn. It's a state that already has a sign on it.

And peer traffic can't produce that. Two parts trading pictures in the same language have nothing at stake between them. There's no boundary being defended in that exchange.

### And a third kind, which is the two of them together

Not a third wire. The *circuit* — one peer connection and one world connection closed into a single loop.

That's what "grounded" actually names in the title. Not the body-connection on its own. The whole closed thing.

Worth being precise about which one does what: **the world connection alone is just a reflex.** Something happens, something responds, nothing carries over. It's the peer connection that turns an arc into a loop.

*Where to go deeper: §2.3. Most load-bearing section in the theory.*

