## The loop

Put it together:

> **You need at least one peer connection and at least one world connection, closed into a single circuit.**

Peer alone: beautiful integration, nothing at stake. A conversation with no consequences.
World alone: a reflex. Something happens, something responds, nothing carries over.
Both: a unified experience that's an experience *of something*, *for someone*.

**Being connected isn't the same as being closed.**

A peer connection only counts if what arrives actually stands out from the receiving side's own background noise. If one hemisphere is generating so much of its own chatter that anything arriving from the other is lost in it, the wiring is intact and the connection is functionally not there. That's roughly what general anesthesia does — not cutting the cable, raising the noise until nothing crossing it registers.

A world connection only counts if what arrives actually limits you. That's the uptake step.

**And there's no screening room.** No place in you where it all comes together and gets watched. There can't be — any spot holding the finished product would be a spot from which the division is invisible, and the division is the requirement. Besides, put a theatre anywhere and you need someone in the seats, and then someone watching *them*.

What holds you together isn't a location. It's the circuit closing. A standing wave on a string doesn't have a position; it's what the whole string is doing.

**One strange consequence, and two later sections run on it:** *you cannot watch your own loop.* Not because you're not trying hard enough. There's nowhere to watch it from. Any seat with a view of the whole thing would be a seat where the split isn't happening.

*Where to go deeper: §§2.4 and 2.5.*

