## The self, in pieces

The part most likely to be recognizable from the inside.

"Who I am" is doing two jobs at once, and each job can be done from either of two positions.

### Two jobs

**Right now** — the coherence of you at this moment. What's running.

**All of it** — your whole history added up. Your account of yourself. The story.

These come apart. You can be scattered this afternoon with your life story perfectly intact. You can have a corrupted or missing history while this moment feels completely normal. **Neither one notices when the other breaks.** That's how you know they're separate.

### Two positions

**From inside** — how things look from where you're standing. The view out.

**From outside** — how you'd look to someone looking *at* you. That's self-image.

### Where the inside view comes from

Something has to force you to be one thing, and here's what does it.

Look at how lopsided you are. Signal pours in from everywhere at once — every patch of skin separately, two ears, two eyes, a gut, a bloodstream, a hundred channels that don't agree and don't arrive together.

And then it all has to come out of one mouth. One body, doing one thing.

You cannot act without picking. Every single time, the crowd of inputs has to become one move, and the picking can't be random — something has to decide which version of you is the one that acts.

**Do that a few million times and the pattern of what you kept picking is who you are.** Not a thing you have. A record of what you committed to, because commitment was never optional.

That's also why "what you value" isn't decoration on identity. Value is the thing doing the picking. It's the machinery, not the flavour.

### Where the outside view comes from

You can't stand outside yourself. And you can't watch your own loop, remember — there's nowhere to watch it from.

But you *can* sit in a conversation.

Two people talking face to face is the same shape as what's inside you. Two comparable parts. A channel carrying the same language both directions. Each one anchored to its own body. It's your architecture, built out of two people instead of two hemispheres — **and you can see this one, because you're only half of it and the other half is sitting right there.**

That's the trick. It's the only place you ever get to watch the thing you are.

### What the conversation gives you

Eye contact is the purest version, and for a specific reason: looking is the one thing you can't do to someone without them being able to do it back through the same channel, at the same instant. You can't hear yourself the way they hear you. You can't touch and be touched in quite the same symmetrical way. But two people looking at each other are each doing exactly the same thing to the other, simultaneously.

That said — this isn't gaze or nothing. People who've been blind since birth build the outside view just a little slower. Conversation without eyes is a lower-bandwidth version of the same shape. Text is lower still. It's a dial, not a switch.
And the conversation gives you two separate things:
A category. You cannot work out from the inside which of your states are yours specifically and which are just what any person does. Nothing to compare against. Other people are the comparison.
That the category has insides. Contrast alone would just tell you other people are very consistent weather. What tells you they're someones is modelling their stakes — and you have to build that yourself, because stakes can't cross a peer channel. There's no setpoint in language. So you run your own machinery over their situation and infer.
That's empathy, and it isn't a nice extra on top of social skill. It's the workaround for something the channel structurally cannot carry.
Which means the damage only runs one way
The inside view has nothing to lose — it's built in. Self-image depends on something arriving.
So anything that interferes with the borrowing takes out the self-image and leaves everything else exactly where it was. Not bad luck. The only direction available.
What that looks like: both jobs still working, both running from the inside, and no person for any of it to be about. Experiences happening, a history intact, nobody there to attribute them to. Awareness completely fine. Self-awareness completely fine — you know perfectly well there's a you. Just no one in the mirror.
If that describes something you recognize, the theory says the architecture is intact and an import is missing. It does not say anything is missing from you in the sense that matters — see the ethical argument two sections up, which is not a consolation, it's the structure.
Why humans get so much of this
Human babies are born early. Not by accident — the brain keeps growing at fetal speed for about a year after birth, which no other ape does.
Which means we do an enormous amount of construction in the one place the outside view is available: out here, in a room, with other people. Other apes do more of that work sealed inside, where there's only one channel and it isn't the right kind.
And we're passed around. Most great ape mothers keep their infants almost entirely to themselves. Human babies get handed to grandmothers, aunts, siblings, neighbours — and have to work out how to engage each of them differently. Not one intense bond. Several distinguishable people, which is the thing that has to be learned.
