Grown systems and built systems
One more distinction, because it decides a lot.
Some things satisfy this whole architecture without being alive. A corporation genuinely has instruments that turn world into its own format. It has a partition — the accounting represents the operations while the operations run. It defends a boundary. It survives every person in it being replaced. And it can discover a problem no individual inside it knew about.
So you can't wave it off. But it isn't the same kind of thing you are:
Grown — your boundary was not your idea. You cannot decide to stop being hungry. Your instruments were inherited.
Built — the system designed its own instruments and can redesign them. Its grounding is borrowed from the grown systems that made it.
Put through the two-step version: a corporation has real uptake — an audit really does change what happens next. What it can't do is establish, because that needs a boundary it didn't write for itself. Half the machinery is authentic, which is exactly why these things are so convincing.
Three consequences worth carrying:
A limit you can rewrite isn't limiting you. So built systems drift toward measuring whatever they can hit rather than whatever actually constrains them — not bad management, architecture. If you've watched an organization slowly optimize for its own metrics until the metrics stopped meaning anything, you've watched this.
Their identity is thin, and now we know why. You became someone because you were forced to pick — many inputs, one mouth. A company has a hundred mouths. It can say incompatible things through different people on the same afternoon and never once has to choose. Nothing forces commitment, so nothing accumulates. The institutions with the strongest identities are the ones that must speak with one voice.
Stakes require the grown kind. Built systems have borrowed stakes — yours, aggregated and bent. Which is exactly why harm done through a company is real harm, without the company itself having anything at stake.
Where to go deeper: §2.12. The theory keeps a written test for throwing it out.
Does this work outside humans?
Short version: the theory says it should, and birds are the best place to check.
Birds have no corpus callosum and nothing like our cortex. Their brains look completely different. But the fine wiring turns out to be built the same way — the same kind of columns, the same crossing pattern of fibres running up-and-down and side-to-side. Different building, same plumbing.
So the theory has a specific job: find the peer channel in a bird. If it's there, the claim that this is about architecture rather than anatomy gets its first real support outside mammals.
One tempting explanation that doesn't survive. It'd be neat if bird smarts came from raising young — parenting requires exactly the sustained interaction the outside view needs. Except crocodiles guard their nests and carry hatchlings in their mouths. Some fish brood. Some frogs carry tadpoles. And there's a bird that buries its eggs in a compost heap and never meets its children, who dig themselves out and walk away.
So parenting isn't it.
What actually sorts the smart ones is years spent around specific individuals you can tell apart and remember. Crows and parrots: long childhoods, lasting partnerships, stable groups where everyone knows everyone. Dolphins get there another way entirely — they're born ready to swim, but they have signature whistles that work like names, and they copy each other's to call to one another.
Not care. Company, over time, with individuals you keep straight.
Where to go deeper: §3.3.
What the theory won't tell you
Worth knowing before you go in, so you're not waiting for it.
What experiences are like. This theory says when a someone is possible. It says nothing about what red is, or why anything feels the way it feels. Different question, not addressed.
How to build one. It's a detector, not a recipe. It states what's necessary, not what's sufficient, so you can't work backwards to a construction manual. Deliberately.
What rights follow. It'll tell you whether something has stakes. It won't tell you what to do about that. Genuinely separate questions, and it refuses the second one on purpose.
Reading the real document
Three parts.
Part 1 describes what any system does with information. Four operations, the movements between them, and what those movements produce once the circle closes. It's been rebuilt in this version and it's where the new material is.
Part 2 works out what closing the circle requires, and everything above comes out of that. This is where the theory lives. §§2.3 and 2.4 are the ones to read closely.
Part 3 looks for the architecture in an actual nervous system and checks it against what happens when you break things — anesthesia, split brains, coma, brain injury.
It argues with itself throughout, and there's a list of open problems at the end that's longer than most people would put their name to. That's not weakness showing. The whole method is "try to destroy it, not defend it," and the flags are the method working.
Before you go
Take a second. Notice that you're the thing this is about. Notice that you can check most of it from where you're sitting — that you are divided, that something does reach you from outside, that you do have stakes in what happens next.
And notice you can't quite catch yourself doing it. That's not a failure of attention. It's the theory being right.
Go read the real one. It's more careful than I've been here, and about half of it is the theory pointing out where it might be wrong.
